  One thing to flag before I ask anything, because it changes the shape of the whole conversation: [1mthe repo is empty.[0m
  [38;5;153mindex.html[39m is 11 lines with an empty [38;5;153m<main>[39m, and that's the entire codebase. There is no task model, no storage, no
  users, no concept of "changes."

  A notifications system presupposes four things that don't exist yet:

  1. [1mTasks[0m — persisted, with fields that can change
  2. [1mUsers[0m — identity, so "tasks they care about" means something
  3. [1mChange events[0m — something that detects and records a task changing
  4. [1mDelivery[0m — where a notification actually shows up

  Notifications are the [3mlast[0m of those four, not the first. If I start building notifications now I'd be inventing tasks,
  users, and a change-event model as a side effect of a feature meant to consume them — which is how you end up with a
  data model shaped by its most peripheral consumer.

  So my working assumption is this decomposes into sub-projects, and notifications is a later one. But before I push
  that, I need to know what you're actually holding in your head, because "this app" implies you have more in mind than
  the 11 lines on disk.
